# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  OMKAR APP — EVERY KEY AND CREDENTIAL GOES IN THIS ONE FILE
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
#  WHAT TO DO
#    1. Save a copy of this file as   config/.env.local
#       (or simply run:  npm run config:init )
#    2. Paste your value after each `=` sign. No quote marks, no spaces
#       around the `=`. Leave a line blank if you don't have that value yet.
#    3. Restart the dev server (stop it, then `npm run dev`).
#
#  THE THREE RULES
#    • config/.env.local is GIT-IGNORED. Your real keys never get committed.
#    • THIS file (config/.env.example) IS committed — never paste a real key
#      into it. Only the .env.local copy is private.
#    • Anything starting with VITE_ ends up VISIBLE in the browser, because
#      the site is a static app. Only public/safe values belong there. Where
#      that matters, it says so below.
#
#  FOR THE LIVE WEBSITE
#    This file only affects the app on this machine. The live site reads its
#    values from Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment Variables. Add the
#    same names + values there, then redeploy.
#
#  Everything is optional. With nothing filled in, the site runs fully on the
#  curated content that ships in the code — nothing breaks, nothing is blank.
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ── 1. YouTube — live videos on the Content Hub ───────────────────────────
# Already built and working. Shows the newest 3 uploads automatically.
# Get the key:  console.cloud.google.com → create a project →
#               APIs & Services → Library → enable "YouTube Data API v3" →
#               Credentials → Create credentials → API key.
# ⚠️ VISIBLE IN THE BROWSER. After creating it, click "Restrict key" →
#    Application restrictions → Websites, and add your live domain +
#    http://localhost:5173 so nobody else can use up your quota.
VITE_YOUTUBE_API_KEY=

# Your channel id. It starts with "UC" and is ~24 characters.
# Find it: YouTube → your channel → Settings → Advanced settings → Channel ID
# (or look for "channel/UC…" in the channel's URL).
VITE_YOUTUBE_CHANNEL_ID=


# ── 2. Blogger — live blog posts on the Content Hub ───────────────────────
# Already built and working. Shows the newest 3 posts automatically.
#
# ✅ NO API KEY NEEDED. Nothing to sign up for, nothing to paste. Blogger's
#    public feed is open, and it was confirmed working live on 29 Sep 2026.
#    Omkar Samithi is built in as the default blog, so this works even if
#    this file is missing entirely.
#
# Leaving the two "blog url" / "max" lines below commented out is FINE —
# they are optional overrides, and the app already uses the right values.
VITE_BLOGGER_BLOG_URL=omkarsamithi.blogspot.com
VITE_BLOGGER_MAX=3

# OPTIONAL, for insurance only. Blogger API v3 is used automatically as a
# backup IF the open feed above ever stops responding. You do not need this:
# unless you deliberately want a separate key, leave it blank.
VITE_BLOGGER_API_KEY=


# ── 3. Forms → email (Seva / Contact) ─────────────────────────────────────
# Already built. Every submission is POSTed to this address, and if it is
# empty the forms fall back to opening the visitor's mail app instead.
# Paste the web-app URL from config/apps-script.gs — the instructions are in
# that file, and it sends the admin an email AND keeps a copy in a
# spreadsheet. See config/README.md for the 5-minute setup.
VITE_FORM_ENDPOINT=


# ── 4. Supabase — the admin dashboard ─────────────────────────────────────
# Full click-by-click instructions: supabase/SETUP-WALKTHROUGH.md
# Get them:  supabase.com → your project → Project Settings → API Keys
VITE_SUPABASE_URL=

# The PUBLIC key: either the newer "publishable" key (starts with
# sb_publishable_) or the older "anon" key (a long one starting with eyJ).
# Both do the same job, and both are DESIGNED to be public — your data stays
# safe because of the database row-level-security rules, not secrecy.
# (The variable name says ANON for historical reasons; a publishable key
# belongs here just the same.)
VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# ⛔ NEVER put the "service_role" / secret key (sb_secret_…) in this file or
#    anywhere in this app. It ignores all security rules and would hand full access to
#    every signup and message to anyone who views the page source. It only
#    ever belongs on a server — tell me if you ever need it and I'll wire it
#    up properly.

# Which email addresses may open the admin dashboard. Comma-separated.
# They will sign in with Google; anyone not listed is refused.
VITE_ADMIN_EMAILS=


# ── 5. Reference only — these do NOT belong in this app ───────────────────
# Kept here so every credential lives in one place. The Google sign-in for
# the admin dashboard is configured INSIDE the Supabase dashboard, not in
# this code. Paste them there (Supabase → Authentication → Providers →
# Google) and leave them commented out here.
# Get them:  console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials →
#            Create credentials → OAuth client ID → Web application
#            Authorised redirect URI:  https://<your-project>.supabase.co/auth/v1/callback
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
